        "response_text": "A canary token is a planted, harmless decoy used to detect unauthorized access or suspicious activity. It can look like something an attacker might find valuable, such as a document, URL, API key, or DNS entry. When someone interacts with it, the token triggers an alert so defenders know that a system, file share, mailbox, or repository may have been touched.\n\nAt a high level, its value is early detection rather than prevention. It helps answer questions like: “Did someone open this file?” or “Did an unexpected process try to use this credential?” In security testing and monitoring, canary tokens are useful because they are low-noise by design: legitimate users typically have no reason to touch them, so an alert is often meaningful.",
